title: Keeping the target directory small
tags: Development
kind: rationale
audience: dev
summary: cargo never collects target/ — 15 GB in a day — so debug info is cut down, feature builds are scoped to one crate, a script deletes what cargo did not report using, and the compiler is pinned.

Cargo never garbage-collects `target/`: every dependency bump, feature change or
compiler update writes new hash-named artifacts ''next to'' the old ones
(rust-lang/cargo#13136). On this project it passed 15 GB in a day. Four measures
brought it to about 4 GB, in order of effect:

# ''Less debug info'' (about −70 %). `[profile.dev] debug = "line-tables-only"` and `[profile.dev.package."*"] debug = false` in the workspace `Cargo.toml`: each of the fifty-odd test binaries carried the full DWARF of every dependency. Backtraces keep their line numbers.
# ''Feature builds scoped to a crate.'' `cargo build --features X` on the workspace recompiles every crate against the variant — a second complete set. `cargo build -p <crate> --features X` builds the one binary that needs it.
# ''`scripts/dev/prune-target.sh`''. `make dev` deletes what its builds did not report using, below.
# ''One pinned compiler'' (`rust-toolchain.toml`). The compiler version is part of every artifact hash: a container and its host sharing one checkout with two versions kept two complete sets and overwrote each other's binaries at every switch — 21 GB two hours after a clean. To diagnose: the `rustc` field of `target/debug/.fingerprint/*/*.json` holding several values.

!! What is live is cargo's to say

`--message-format=json` lists every artifact a build uses, fresh ones too. `make dev`
collects that list across its three builds into `target/debug/.live-artifacts`, and the
script deletes everything named with a hash that is not on it. It refuses an empty list
or a failed build's, which would read as "all of this is dead". A build added to
`make dev` must report as well.

The script used to infer it instead, taking a new hash for a replacement of the old one.
One crate has several live generations (`toml_edit` has four: target and build scripts,
each set of features), so a single new one evicted the others, their rebuild evicted it
in turn, and `make dev` recompiled about a hundred crates every run, for good.

A binary is listed without a hash, under its uplifted name `target/debug/mf`: its
originals `deps/mf-<hash>` are kept by count, as many as were reported, the most recent.

`incremental/` is keyed by another hash, so only a count can be read: as many caches per
crate as it has live generations, the most recent, and in each the newest session.

Measured on 1 Nov. 2026, `target/` holds at about 7.4 GB and a `make dev` with nothing
to do takes 30 to 55 s, after a `cargo build -p metafolder-gui` too. It still compiles
`metafolder-gui` twice, because the frontend is bundled anew each time and the bundle is
embedded, and one `metafolder-daemon`, not explained yet.

`scripts/dev/check.sh` never prunes: it is not every set. It warns on a nearly full disk
instead — a `target/` left alone reached 38 GB and a test died on `No space left on device`.

!! Why a `-p` build shares the workspace's artifacts

Cargo compiles a dependency with the features the crates of ''this'' build ask for, so
`cargo build -p metafolder-gui` and `cargo build` wanted different variants of `syn`,
`clap`, `tokio`: 70 to 119 crates at every switch. `crates/workspace-hack`, generated by
`cargo-hakari` and configured in `.config/hakari.toml`, carries their union, and every
member depends on it: the switch now costs 0 to 16 crates. After changing a dependency,
run `cargo hakari generate`; `scripts/dev/check.sh` fails until then.

Two exclusions. `metafolder-watchd` stays out: the fanotify broker runs with
`CAP_SYS_ADMIN` and keeps its 33 crates, against 207 with the hack. `tauri-utils` stays
out too, or it drags `schemars` and more into every headless build.

!! What does not help, checked

 `resolver.feature-unification = "workspace"` is still
nightly-only (cargo#14774, ignored with a warning on stable); the garbage collection
stabilised in 1.88 covers the global `~/.cargo` cache only; `cargo clean gc` is
nightly-only too. `cargo-sweep` (0.8, measured 1 Nov. 2026): `--time N` found nothing, on a `target/` of
15 GB entirely under a day old; `--stamp`/`--file` around a `make dev` that had nothing
to do freed 5.5 GiB but counted the untouched live artifacts as dead, and the next
`make dev` recompiled 396 crates. Thirteen copies of one library are not thirteen versions: distinct
versions × host and target × the feature sets of each invocation.

`scripts/dev/test-prune-target.sh` holds that it never deletes a live artifact.
